From 0b0df50f31e5a795d7298517e5fc7d1b94a2795b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 03:45:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] kanban: route authority recovery through
 authenticated owner-wake tool

Problem: the first authority-reopen patch passed helper tests but was unreachable
from a fenced worker shell and looked up a default-board supervisor on bb.
Why: CLI entry fences reject worker shell writes before the handler; workers'
terminal environments intentionally lack task/run authority.
Root (happened): the recovery privilege was implemented below the authenticated
tool boundary rather than composing the existing cross-board owner-wake grant.
Root (not caught): private-handler same-board fixtures bypassed both boundaries.
Recurrence: follow-up to cb3a7b1f, independently rejected on review card
t_eeeeee000000003c; no shell fence exception is retained.

Countermeasure: explicit kanban_unblock(task_id=<listed contained target>,
board=<listed board>, reopen_authority=<exact nominated done authority>) uses
worker authority on its own board and containment evidence on the target board.
Revalidate the live run and policy inside reopen's write transaction, locking
the source board during cross-board mutation. Ordinary reopen parent gating
and audit remain in place; containment release and shell permissions unchanged.

Proof: public-tool regression 12 passed, including cross-board and same-board
success, unrelated authority/target/board, non-owner, stale/ended/dead run,
delegated child, direct containment resume and shell refusals. Mutant disabling
the new tool branch fails both success cases (2 failed). Both episodes and the
consumer dependency remain held in positive tests. Portable checks, independent
review, exact-head CI and canonical ship remain mandatory before delivery.
